# Day 08: PowerShell Remoting

## Overview

On Day 08 of my PowerShell 10 Days: Zero to Hero challenge, I focused on PowerShell Remoting using Windows Server 2022.

The goal of this practical session was to understand how PowerShell Remoting enables System Administrators to execute commands remotely, interact with remote sessions, collect system information, and perform remote administration using WinRM.

## Key Learning Areas

- WinRM Configuration and Verification
- WSMan Connectivity Testing
- Interactive Remote Sessions
- Remote Command Execution
- Remote System Information Collection
- Remote Service Monitoring
- Remote Process Monitoring

## Hands-on Practical Exercises

### Lab 1 – WinRM & Remoting Setup

Check WinRM service status, inspect session configurations, verify WinRM configuration, and test WSMan connectivity.

```powershell
Get-Service WinRM

Get-PSSessionConfiguration

winrm get winrm/config

Test-WSMan localhost
```

### Lab 2 – Enter-PSSession (Interactive Remote Session)

Establish an interactive remote session and perform basic system checks inside the remote session.

```powershell
Enter-PSSession -ComputerName localhost

hostname

Get-Service WinRM

Get-CimInstance Win32_OperatingSystem |
Select-Object Caption, Version

Exit-PSSession
```

### Lab 3 – Invoke-Command (Remote Command Execution)

Execute commands remotely to retrieve hostname, operating system information, WinRM status, and process information.

```powershell
Invoke-Command -ComputerName localhost -ScriptBlock {
    hostname
}

Invoke-Command -ComputerName localhost -ScriptBlock {
    Get-CimInstance Win32_OperatingSystem |
    Select-Object Caption, Version
}

Invoke-Command -ComputerName localhost -ScriptBlock {
    Get-Service WinRM
}

Invoke-Command -ComputerName localhost -ScriptBlock {
    Get-Process |
    Select-Object -First 10 Name, Id, CPU
}
```

### Lab 4 – Remote Administration

Collect system information, retrieve running services, and monitor processes through a single remote ScriptBlock.

```powershell
Invoke-Command -ComputerName localhost -ScriptBlock {

    Get-CimInstance Win32_OperatingSystem |
    Select-Object Caption, Version

    Get-Service |
    Where-Object Status -eq "Running" |
    Select-Object -First 10 Name, Status

    Get-Process |
    Sort-Object CPU -Descending |
    Select-Object -First 10 Name, Id, CPU
}
```

